 📝 Hashing Summary

 🔹 What is Hashing?
 Hashing: Converts input data to a fixed-size hash value using a hash function.
 Hash Function: Maps a key to an index.
 Hash Table: Stores key-value pairs using hashing for fast access.

 🔹 Key Concepts:
 Collisions: Occur when two keys hash to the same index.

 Collision Resolution:
   Chaining (linked list or tree)
   Open Addressing (linear/quadratic probing)
 Load Factor: When exceeded, triggers rehashing.
 Cryptographic Hashing: Secure, one-way, deterministic, collision-resistant.

 🧩 HashMap Summary

 ✅ Features:

 Stores key-value pairs.
 Allows one null key and multiple null values.
 Unordered (not sorted or insertion order guaranteed).
 Uses hashing to store keys and resolve collisions.

 ⚙️ Common Methods & Examples:

| Method                    | Example                                               |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| put(K key, V value)     | map.put("apple", 100);                              |
| get(Object key)         | map.get("apple"); // returns 100                    |
| remove(Object key)      | map.remove("apple");                                |
| containsKey(Object k)   | map.containsKey("apple"); // true/false             |
| containsValue(Object v) | map.containsValue(100); // true/false               |
| keySet()                | Set<String> keys = map.keySet();                    |
| values()                | Collection<Integer> vals = map.values();            |
| entrySet()              | for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> e : map.entrySet()) |

 🌱 HashSet Summary

 ✅ Features:

 Stores unique elements.
 Backed internally by a HashMap.
 Only one null element allowed.
 No duplicates, no guaranteed order.

 ⚙️ Common Methods & Examples:

| Method               | Example                                |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| add(E e)           | set.add("apple");                    |
| remove(Object o)   | set.remove("apple");                 |
| contains(Object o) | set.contains("apple"); // true/false |
| size()             | int n = set.size();                  |
| clear()            | set.clear();                         |
| isEmpty()          | set.isEmpty(); // true/false         |
| iterator()         | for (String s : set) {}              |

 🔍 Short Code Examples of Each Method

 ✅ HashMap Examples

Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put("apple", 10);
System.out.println(map.get("apple"));           // 10
map.remove("apple");
System.out.println(map.containsKey("apple"));   // false

map.put("banana", 20);
System.out.println(map.containsValue(20));      // true

Set<String> keys = map.keySet();                // [banana]
Collection<Integer> values = map.values();      // [20]

for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> e : map.entrySet()) {
    System.out.println(e.getKey() + " -> " + e.getValue());
}


 ✅ HashSet Examples

Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

set.add("apple");
set.add("banana");
System.out.println(set.contains("apple"));      // true
set.remove("apple");
System.out.println(set.contains("apple"));      // false

System.out.println(set.size());                 // 1
set.clear();
System.out.println(set.isEmpty());              // true

set.add("cherry");
for (String s : set) {
    System.out.println(s);
}



Difference:

| Feature                | `HashMap<K, V>`                                   | `HashSet<E>`                                     |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Purpose            	   | Stores key-value pairs                        	   | Stores unique elements                       	   |
| Internal Structure 	   | Backed by array of buckets with `Node<K, V>`  	   | Internally uses a `HashMap<E, Object>`       	   |
| Duplicates Allowed 	   | Keys must be unique, values can be duplicated 	   | No duplicates allowed                        	   |
| Null Support       	   | Allows one null key and multiple null values  	   | Allows only one null element                 	   |
| Methods            	   | `put(key, value)`, `get(key)`, `remove(key)`      | `add(value)`, `contains(value)`, `remove(value)` |
| Key Operations     	   | Retrieve/update values via keys                   | Add/remove/check existence of values             |
| Underlying Usage   	   | Used when mapping one value to another            | Used when checking existence only            	   |
| Entry Example      	   | `{"apple" → 100}`                                 | `["apple"]`                                      |

